// HumanityOS ship structure: MANY pressurized zones, each a fixed outer box + freely
// placed interior walls (the proven home model, now plural -- increments A + B of
// docs/design/ship-superstructure.md). Each zone: id, label, purpose
// (residence|commons|bay|agriculture|corridor), a world origin for its box min corner
// (y = deck height), and the full home body in zone-local metres. The former
// data/blueprints/home_structure.ron migrated outright into zone 'home' below.
// `corridors` (bottom of the file) generates straight walk-through tubes between
// two zones. Each row OWNS its door mouths: lat = the tube centreline's WORLD
// coordinate on the axis across the run (world z for an X run, world x for a Z run);
// door_width/door_height = the aperture the corridor cuts through each zone's shell.
// Corridors never reference authored door walls -- do not author a wall flush with a
// corridor mouth (two coplanar walls z-fight and one of them has no collision).
//
// AI + human friendly: add a zone by adding an entry to zones; add a wall by adding an
// InteriorWall line inside a zone body. The construction editor edits the same data
// (zone selector at the top of the Home structure panel).
//
// Materials: 0 = grid, 1 = steel/metal, 2 = concrete, 3 = wood, 4 = glass.
//
// Editor saves rewrite everything BELOW this header from the loaded struct: body comments
// are dropped, floats are re-printed (0.70 -> 0.7), and any field the running build does not
// know is dropped too (a build older than `room_type` strips every room_type on save;
// `just validate-data` then fails loudly, so run it after a save from an older build).
// Durable notes belong here or in docs/design/homestead.md, not beside the records.
//
// Zones: `room_type: Some("<data/rooms.ron key>")` on a room-grade zone gives the room the
// zone covers its FUNCTION (purpose, walk-up actions, access); rooms no zone covers stay
// room_N. Since the 2026-09-19 redesign every room in the player's acre is covered, so a
// `room_N` id appearing again means a wall stopped short and two rooms merged.
//
// THE PLAYER'S ACRE (zone "home", 55 x 89 m, 1.21 acres). Before the redesign 330 m2 of it
// was partitioned and the other 4,565 m2 was one open hall used as a tech-demo floor. It is
// now 23 rooms in four bands along z, with the house in the middle so nothing is a long walk:
//
//   z  0..22   the yard      power terrace (x 0..20) | vehicle bay (x 20..55)
//   z 22..30   the belt      service way (x 0..34)   | orchard court (x 34..55)
//   z 30..51   the middle    plant room (0..12) | forge (12..20) | workshop (20..34) | HOUSE (34..55)
//   z 51..73   the growing   mushroom room + aquaponics (x 0..10) | greenhouse (x 10..55)
//   z 73..89   the far end   fields (x 0..40) | barn (x 40..55)
//
// The house is a 21 x 21 m square entered from the ship's corridor at x=55, z 39..41: an
// entry, a hall across the middle, the quiet rooms north facing the orchard court, and the
// living rooms south behind a glass wall onto the greenhouse. The CONSOLE ROOM (colloquially
// the battlestation; the mothership bridge is the "command deck") is zone "console-room" at
// x 52..55, z 43..51: a narrow den off the entry and the great room, glazed at its south end
// so the garden is behind the screens. `scripts/verify-screens.js` parks there.
//
// Why these numbers: 55 and 89 are consecutive Fibonacci, so the shell is already a golden
// rectangle, and 34/21/22/16 and 13/8/5/3 tile it with no offcuts. The full reasoning, the
// walkthrough, and what each screen is doing where it is: docs/design/player-home.md.

